\label{sec:rrmoa}

An \emph{adapter} $g_\phi: \mathbb{R}^{B \times T \times d} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^{B \times h}$ maps a pretrained backbone's hidden states (batch $B$, sequence length $T$, hidden dim $d$) to forecasts of horizon $h$, with $n_g \leq 500{,}000$ trainable parameters. (We use lowercase $h$ for forecast horizon to disambiguate from $\mathbf{H}_\mathrm{FM}$ below, the backbone hidden state.) The goal of per-window routing is to select, for each input window, the subset of expert adapters best suited to that window's characteristics, so that a quiet baseline and a volatile spike are handled by different adapter topologies rather than forced through the same head. As shown in the introduction, this fails when the router reads normalized hidden states, because RevIN removes the per-window statistics that distinguish input regimes. \textbf{RR-MoA} avoids this by splitting the information into two streams: the frozen TSFM produces deep embeddings $\mathbf{H}$ for the experts, while a separate lightweight gate reads the \textit{raw, pre-normalization} input $\mathbf{X}_\text{raw}$ to decide which experts to activate:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{Y} &= \sum_{j=1}^{K} \widetilde{w}_j \cdot \text{Expert}_j(\mathbf{H}_\text{FM}), \qquad
\mathcal{T} = \mathrm{TopK}\!\bigl(G_\psi(\mathbf{X}_\text{raw}),\, k\bigr), \\
\widetilde{w}_j &= \begin{cases}
\dfrac{\exp\!\bigl(G_\psi(\mathbf{X}_\text{raw})_j\bigr)}{\sum_{j'\in\mathcal{T}}\exp\!\bigl(G_\psi(\mathbf{X}_\text{raw})_{j'}\bigr)}, & j\in\mathcal{T},\\[4pt]
0, & j\notin\mathcal{T}.
\end{cases}
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:rrmoa}
\end{equation}
where $G_\psi: \mathbb{R}^{T} \to \mathbb{R}^K$ is a small Conv1d\,+\,pooling\,+\,linear gate ($853$ params) operating directly on the raw time series, $\mathcal{T} \subset \{1,\ldots,K\}$ is the set of Top-$k$ expert indices, and each $\mathrm{Expert}_j$ is a canonical adapter head. By construction $\sum_j \widetilde{w}_j = 1$ with support only on $\mathcal{T}$, so only $k$ of $K$ experts contribute per sample (the remaining $K{-}k$ are masked out and do not execute). With $K{=}5$, $k{=}2$ we obtain $40\%$ of the dense ($k{=}K$) expert FLOPs while retaining $63\%$ of the dense MSE improvement over the best fixed adapter (\S\ref{sec:topk}). The raw input preserves the temporal statistics (trend, amplitude, volatility) that RevIN strips away, enabling per-sample routing that reflects the physical characteristics of each time series window.

\textbf{Expert pool.} We maintain $K{=}5$ topologically distinct expert adapters (mean-pool, last-token, max-pool, attention-pool, Conv1d-pool), spanning the canonical pooling families used in sequence representation learning~\citep{reimers2019sentencebert}. Architectural diversity is critical: replacing the five distinct heads with five identical experts (same topology, different initialization) degrades MSE by $8$--$15\%$ (Appendix~\ref{app:diversity}). Pseudocode in Algorithm~\ref{alg:rrmoa} (Appendix~\ref{app:setup}). We now empirically validate that this minimal fix recovers routing diversity, beats the comprehensive baseline suite, and that its predictions hold under eight causal controls.

\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Setup}

\textbf{Datasets and tasks.} Six LTSF benchmarks (ETTh1/h2/m1/m2~\citep{zhou2021informer}, Weather, Electricity~\citep{godahewa2021monash}) with chronological splits and channel-wise normalization~\citep{nie2023patchtst}. We evaluate forecasting ($H{=}96$) and imputation ($20\%$ masked).

\textbf{Backbones.} Five backbones spanning two normalization families: instance-normalized MOMENT-small/large~\citep{goswami2024moment} (internal RevIN~\citep{kim2021revin}); LayerNorm-only Moirai~\citep{woo2024moirai} and Moirai-MoE; and Chronos~\citep{ansari2024chronos} and Timer-XL~\citep{liu2025timerxl} as no-instance-normalization negative controls (\S\ref{sec:cross_backbone}). MOMENT-small is primary. Primary results use a strictly frozen backbone; freeze-level ablations follow.

\textbf{Baselines.} Seven baselines: three fixed adapters, LoRA~\citep{hu2022lora} (108-run sweep; Appendix~\ref{app:lora_sweep}), TRACE~\citep{li2025trace}, an independent ensemble of 5 experts, AdaMix~\citep{wang2022adamix} (hidden-state MoE), full fine-tuning (all blocks unfrozen, best of 5 heads $\times$ 2 LRs), and a from-scratch DLinear~\citep{zeng2023dlinear} calibration anchor.

\textbf{Training and reproducibility.} Adam, MSE loss, 15 epochs; 5 seeds for the core grid, 3 for ablations. Backbone dimensions, full hyperparameters, and per-baseline configurations: Appendix~\ref{app:setup}. All code, raw JSON results, routing-weight logs, and a self-verification script (\texttt{verify.py}) that re-derives every numerical claim from raw data accompany the submission.

\subsection{Main Results}
\label{sec:main_results}

RR-MoA's case rests on three claims: that fixed-topology adapters are suboptimal, that the natural MoE remedy collapses on instance-normalized backbones, and that the collapse cannot be recovered by standard rescue mechanisms. We establish each in turn before comparing RR-MoA against a comprehensive baseline suite.

